votes.reasoning:
The new `degreeIncrease` changes the degree in the wrong direction:

```cpp
template <class T>
void gsKnotVector<T>::degreeIncrease(int const & i)
{
    my->p -= i;          // should be my->p += i
    increaseMultFirst(i);
    increaseMultLast (i);
}
```

- **Wrong sign.** `degreeIncrease(i)` should raise the degree by `i`, but `my->p -= i` lowers it. It mirrors `degreeDecrease`, which calls `trim(i)` and then `my->p -= i`. The increase case should use `+=`.
- **Inconsistent state.** Adding `i` to the first and last knot multiplicities matches a degree raised by `i`, as in an open/clamped knot vector, which needs end multiplicity `p+1`. With `p` lowered instead, the end multiplicities become `p + 1 + 2i` relative to the stored degree. That breaks the clamping invariant, and any later code that relies on `p` will misbehave (basis evaluation, `size() - p - 1` coefficient counts, `degreeElevate`'s trimming). For example, calling `degreeIncrease(1)` and then `degreeDecrease(1)` should restore the original knot vector. Instead, `p` ends up 2 lower than it started.

The order of operations (changing `p` before `increaseMultFirst/Last`) only matters if those helpers read `p`. The sign error is a clear defect either way.

Fix:
```cpp
my->p += i;
increaseMultFirst(i);
increaseMultLast (i);
```

